WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.597 --> 00:00:03.290
(whoosh)

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:04.382
- Hello, Believe Nation.

00:00:04.382 --> 00:00:06.073
I started the Did You Know series

00:00:06.073 --> 00:00:07.395
to try to learn a little bit more

00:00:07.395 --> 00:00:09.369
about some of our favorite entrepreneurs,

00:00:09.369 --> 00:00:11.685
what makes them tick, what
their upbringing was like.

00:00:11.685 --> 00:00:14.464
And so, today we're going to
learn nine surprising facts

00:00:14.464 --> 00:00:16.253
about Steve Jobs.

00:00:16.253 --> 00:00:17.568
(bell chimes)

00:00:17.568 --> 00:00:20.823
Fact number one, he was adopted.

00:00:20.823 --> 00:00:23.669
- I dropped out of Reed College
after the first six months,

00:00:23.669 --> 00:00:25.403
but then stayed around as a drop-in

00:00:25.403 --> 00:00:29.305
for another 18 months or
so before I really quit.

00:00:29.305 --> 00:00:30.972
So why'd I drop out?

00:00:31.986 --> 00:00:35.141
It started before I was born.

00:00:35.141 --> 00:00:39.126
My biological mother was a
young, unwed graduate student,

00:00:39.126 --> 00:00:42.006
and she decided to put me up for adoption.

00:00:42.006 --> 00:00:43.647
She felt very strongly

00:00:43.647 --> 00:00:46.055
that I should be adopted
by college graduates,

00:00:46.055 --> 00:00:48.863
so everything was all set
for me to be adopted at birth

00:00:48.863 --> 00:00:51.395
by a lawyer and his wife.

00:00:51.395 --> 00:00:53.515
Except that when I popped out,

00:00:53.515 --> 00:00:54.614
they decided at the last minute

00:00:54.614 --> 00:00:57.434
that they really wanted a girl.

00:00:57.434 --> 00:01:00.016
So, my parents, who
were on a waiting list,

00:01:00.016 --> 00:01:02.845
got a call in the middle
of the night asking,

00:01:02.845 --> 00:01:05.166
"We've got an unexpected baby boy.

00:01:05.166 --> 00:01:07.345
"Do you want him?"

00:01:07.345 --> 00:01:09.262
They said, "Of course."

00:01:10.778 --> 00:01:13.014
My biological mother found out later

00:01:13.014 --> 00:01:15.754
that my mother had never
graduated from college,

00:01:15.754 --> 00:01:18.917
and that my father had never
graduated from high school.

00:01:18.917 --> 00:01:23.207
She refused to sign the
final adoption papers.

00:01:23.207 --> 00:01:25.106
She only relented a few months later,

00:01:25.106 --> 00:01:29.189
when my parents promised
that I would go to college.

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:32.305
This was the start in my life.

00:01:32.305 --> 00:01:36.505
When I was looking for
my biological mother,

00:01:36.505 --> 00:01:38.188
obviously, y'know,

00:01:38.188 --> 00:01:42.166
I was looking for my biological
father at the same time.

00:01:42.166 --> 00:01:45.775
And I learned a little bit about him,

00:01:45.775 --> 00:01:48.086
and I didn't like what I learned,

00:01:48.086 --> 00:01:50.669
and I asked her to not tell him

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:56.264
that we'd ever met, and not
tell him anything about me.

00:01:56.264 --> 00:01:59.265
- So, Mona goes to the coffee shop,

00:01:59.265 --> 00:02:02.039
meets this guy, Mr.
Jindali, who's running it,

00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:04.441
who says, among other
things, when she asks,

00:02:04.441 --> 00:02:07.218
how sorry he is, but then he says

00:02:07.218 --> 00:02:09.989
that he had had another child,

00:02:09.989 --> 00:02:11.866
and Mona said, "What happened to him?"

00:02:11.866 --> 00:02:12.775
He said, "Oh, I don't know,

00:02:12.775 --> 00:02:14.385
"we'll never hear from him again."

00:02:14.385 --> 00:02:18.294
And then he says, "I
wish you could've seen me

00:02:18.294 --> 00:02:20.533
"when I was running a bigger restaurant.

00:02:20.533 --> 00:02:22.509
"I used to run one of the best restaurants

00:02:22.509 --> 00:02:23.559
"in Silicon Valley.

00:02:23.559 --> 00:02:25.575
"Everybody used to come there.

00:02:25.575 --> 00:02:28.610
"Even Steve Jobs used to eat there."

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:30.423
And Mona's sort of taken aback,

00:02:30.423 --> 00:02:31.918
and bites her tongue and doesn't say,

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:34.342
"Steve Jobs is your son."

00:02:34.342 --> 00:02:35.914
But she looked shocked, and he says,

00:02:35.914 --> 00:02:37.799
"Yeah, he was a great tipper."

00:02:37.799 --> 00:02:40.928
- And I was in that
restaurant once or twice,

00:02:40.928 --> 00:02:44.879
and I remember meeting the
owner, who was from Syria,

00:02:44.879 --> 00:02:47.326
and it was most certainly him.

00:02:47.326 --> 00:02:49.574
And I shook his hand,
and he shook my hand,

00:02:49.574 --> 00:02:51.607
and that's all.

00:02:51.607 --> 00:02:54.410
- And Jobs never spoke to
him, never talked to him,

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:57.214
never got in touch with him,
never wanted to see him.

00:02:57.214 --> 00:02:58.600
(bell chimes)

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:01.042
- Fact number two, when
he was 12 years old,

00:03:01.042 --> 00:03:03.259
he got a summer job with Hewlett-Packard.

00:03:03.259 --> 00:03:04.796
- But I called up Bill Hewlett

00:03:04.796 --> 00:03:07.344
when I was 12 years old,
and he lived in Paolo Alto.

00:03:07.344 --> 00:03:09.029
His number was still in the phone book.

00:03:09.029 --> 00:03:10.043
And he answered the phone himself.

00:03:10.043 --> 00:03:10.896
He was like, "Yes?"

00:03:10.896 --> 00:03:12.481
I said, "Hi, I'm Steve
Jobs, I'm 12 years old.

00:03:12.481 --> 00:03:15.052
"I'm a student in high school,

00:03:15.052 --> 00:03:17.067
"and I want to build a frequency counter,

00:03:17.067 --> 00:03:18.391
"and I was wondering if you had

00:03:18.391 --> 00:03:20.376
"any spare parts I could have."

00:03:20.376 --> 00:03:22.873
And he laughed, and he
gave me the spare parts

00:03:22.873 --> 00:03:23.980
to build this frequency counter,

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:26.253
and he gave me a job that
summer in Hewlett-Packard,

00:03:26.253 --> 00:03:27.437
working on the assembly line,

00:03:27.437 --> 00:03:30.421
putting nuts and bolts
together on frequency counters.

00:03:30.421 --> 00:03:32.421
He got me a job in the
place that built them,

00:03:32.421 --> 00:03:33.459
and I was in heaven.

00:03:33.459 --> 00:03:36.921
And I've never found anyone who's said no

00:03:36.921 --> 00:03:39.368
or hung up the phone when I called.

00:03:39.368 --> 00:03:40.771
I just asked.

00:03:40.771 --> 00:03:42.946
And when people ask me, I
try to be as responsive,

00:03:42.946 --> 00:03:45.863
to pay that debt of gratitude back.

00:03:48.657 --> 00:03:50.245
Most people never pick
up the phone and call,

00:03:50.245 --> 00:03:51.159
most people never ask,

00:03:51.159 --> 00:03:53.209
and that's what separates, sometimes,

00:03:53.209 --> 00:03:54.222
the people that do things

00:03:54.222 --> 00:03:56.737
from the people that
just dream about them.

00:03:56.737 --> 00:03:58.553
You've got to act,

00:03:58.553 --> 00:04:01.553
and you've got to be willing to fail.

00:04:03.156 --> 00:04:04.994
You've got to be willing
to crash and burn,

00:04:04.994 --> 00:04:06.360
you know, with people on the phone,

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:08.120
with starting a company, with whatever.

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:08.984
If you're afraid of failing,

00:04:08.984 --> 00:04:11.359
you won't get very far.

00:04:11.359 --> 00:04:12.738
(bell chimes)

00:04:12.738 --> 00:04:15.430
- Fact number three, he
was a college dropout.

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:17.477
- But I naively chose a college

00:04:17.477 --> 00:04:20.832
that was almost as expensive as Stanford,

00:04:20.832 --> 00:04:22.981
and all of my working
class parents' savings

00:04:22.981 --> 00:04:25.799
were being spent on my college tuition.

00:04:25.799 --> 00:04:29.018
After six months, I couldn't
see the value in it.

00:04:29.018 --> 00:04:31.752
I had no idea what I
wanted to do with my life,

00:04:31.752 --> 00:04:32.985
and no idea how college

00:04:32.985 --> 00:04:35.112
was going to help me figure it out.

00:04:35.112 --> 00:04:37.077
And here I was, spending all of the money

00:04:37.077 --> 00:04:40.841
my parents had saved their entire life.

00:04:40.841 --> 00:04:42.694
So I decided to drop out

00:04:42.694 --> 00:04:45.885
and trust that it would all work out okay.

00:04:45.885 --> 00:04:47.487
It was pretty scary at the time,

00:04:47.487 --> 00:04:48.662
but looking back,

00:04:48.662 --> 00:04:51.172
it was one of the best
decisions I ever made.

00:04:51.172 --> 00:04:52.517
(crowd laughs)

00:04:52.517 --> 00:04:53.817
The minute I dropped out,

00:04:53.817 --> 00:04:56.120
I could stop taking the required classes

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:57.868
that didn't interest me,

00:04:57.868 --> 00:04:59.690
and begin dropping in on the ones

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:02.974
that looked far more interesting.

00:05:02.974 --> 00:05:04.829
It wasn't all romantic.

00:05:04.829 --> 00:05:06.061
I didn't have a dorm room,

00:05:06.061 --> 00:05:08.847
so I slept on the floor in friends' rooms.

00:05:08.847 --> 00:05:11.307
I returned Coke bottles
for the five cent deposits

00:05:11.307 --> 00:05:12.903
to buy food with,

00:05:12.903 --> 00:05:14.874
and I would walk the
seven miles across town

00:05:14.874 --> 00:05:16.630
every Sunday night

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:20.435
to get one good meal a week
at the Hari Krishna Temple.

00:05:20.435 --> 00:05:22.060
I loved it.

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:23.928
And much of what I stumbled into

00:05:23.928 --> 00:05:25.974
by following my curiosity and intuition

00:05:25.974 --> 00:05:28.403
turned out to be priceless later on.

00:05:28.403 --> 00:05:29.561
(bell chimes)

00:05:29.561 --> 00:05:30.394
- Fact number four,

00:05:30.394 --> 00:05:31.925
he experimented with psychedelics

00:05:31.925 --> 00:05:34.253
and said it was a profound experience.

00:05:34.253 --> 00:05:36.196
- My favorite Steve anecdote was

00:05:36.196 --> 00:05:38.057
one day just sitting in my office

00:05:38.057 --> 00:05:39.737
and he comes by and he says,

00:05:39.737 --> 00:05:43.261
"Hey, Trip, you've never
taken LSD, have you?"

00:05:43.261 --> 00:05:44.410
(reporter laughs)

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:46.465
And I said, "No."

00:05:46.465 --> 00:05:48.278
And he said, "Yeah, I thought so."

00:05:48.278 --> 00:05:49.755
And then he walks away.

00:05:49.755 --> 00:05:52.092
So, of course, he and I
understood each other well enough

00:05:52.092 --> 00:05:53.884
that he knew that I knew

00:05:53.884 --> 00:05:55.348
that what he's basically doing is saying,

00:05:55.348 --> 00:05:56.946
"I just came out of a
meeting with somebody,

00:05:56.946 --> 00:05:58.642
"and they said that you disagree with me

00:05:58.642 --> 00:06:00.732
"in this way about this thing,

00:06:00.732 --> 00:06:02.479
"and I'm just thinking to myself,

00:06:02.479 --> 00:06:04.231
"that if you had just dropped acid,

00:06:04.231 --> 00:06:06.929
"you wouldn't be so damn screwed up."

00:06:06.929 --> 00:06:09.374
(both laughing)

00:06:09.374 --> 00:06:11.449
Because he really absolutely believed

00:06:11.449 --> 00:06:14.137
that LSD had been a very
positive transformation

00:06:14.137 --> 00:06:15.438
in how he thought.

00:06:15.438 --> 00:06:18.064
- You used to do LSD with Steve Jobs.

00:06:18.064 --> 00:06:21.705
Can you take me back
to those college days?

00:06:21.705 --> 00:06:25.071
Let's just rewind and go back there.

00:06:25.071 --> 00:06:28.297
- Let's see, were we playing Sgt. Pepper?

00:06:28.297 --> 00:06:29.537
- What were you playing?

00:06:29.537 --> 00:06:31.287
How did this go down?

00:06:32.759 --> 00:06:34.302
- It was pretty prosaic.

00:06:34.302 --> 00:06:36.385
We were in Portland at Reed College.

00:06:36.385 --> 00:06:38.691
You know, freshman college year is

00:06:38.691 --> 00:06:42.085
a very poignant time of life where you're

00:06:42.085 --> 00:06:44.792
very much trying to figure
out what the world is about

00:06:44.792 --> 00:06:46.132
and what you're interested in.

00:06:46.132 --> 00:06:48.375
- How'd you guys meet?

00:06:48.375 --> 00:06:50.498
- Oh, well at Reed,

00:06:50.498 --> 00:06:52.555
Reed was a pastoral environment

00:06:52.555 --> 00:06:55.756
and so there was a lot
of hanging out going on,

00:06:55.756 --> 00:06:58.057
but Steve and I developed a friendship

00:06:58.057 --> 00:07:00.951
when we figured out that we had both read

00:07:00.951 --> 00:07:04.406
this amazing book called "Be Here Now,"

00:07:04.406 --> 00:07:07.073
which is about psychedelics
and spirituality.

00:07:07.073 --> 00:07:10.761
Steve was my best friend
at the time of life

00:07:10.761 --> 00:07:12.382
when I was discovering all this

00:07:12.382 --> 00:07:15.184
huge current of Eastern literature.

00:07:15.184 --> 00:07:16.338
All of a sudden,

00:07:16.338 --> 00:07:19.938
psychedelics were being
introduced into the mix

00:07:19.938 --> 00:07:22.171
of traditional spirituality,

00:07:22.171 --> 00:07:23.806
and that was just very fascinating.

00:07:23.806 --> 00:07:24.639
- Do you remember the first time

00:07:24.639 --> 00:07:26.941
you guys took psychedelics together?

00:07:26.941 --> 00:07:28.618
- No, not really.

00:07:28.618 --> 00:07:30.611
We were just kind of walkin' around.

00:07:30.611 --> 00:07:33.342
I think we used to go for hikes.

00:07:33.342 --> 00:07:36.180
I think we camped out on the beach.

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:40.441
I can't really remember
building a campfire.

00:07:40.441 --> 00:07:43.599
I don't remember what
we did when it got dark.

00:07:43.599 --> 00:07:44.888
I can tell you that

00:07:44.888 --> 00:07:48.053
the times that I was taking
psychedelics with Steve,

00:07:48.053 --> 00:07:50.799
we weren't really talking that much.

00:07:50.799 --> 00:07:53.567
We were more of in a meditative space.

00:07:53.567 --> 00:07:55.192
- Once you were there,

00:07:55.192 --> 00:07:59.008
did you and Steve Jobs ever take LSD

00:07:59.008 --> 00:08:01.095
or continue to take psychedelics?

00:08:01.095 --> 00:08:02.207
- Once Apple started,

00:08:02.207 --> 00:08:04.127
Steve was really focused

00:08:04.127 --> 00:08:06.438
with all of his energy

00:08:06.438 --> 00:08:08.688
on making Apple successful.

00:08:09.525 --> 00:08:11.663
And he didn't need psychedelics for that.

00:08:11.663 --> 00:08:13.064
(bell chimes)

00:08:13.064 --> 00:08:14.110
- Fact number five,

00:08:14.110 --> 00:08:16.417
he was fired from Apple.

00:08:16.417 --> 00:08:17.888
- As you may know,

00:08:17.888 --> 00:08:21.805
I was basically fired
from Apple when I was 30,

00:08:23.843 --> 00:08:26.318
and was invited to come
back 12 years later,

00:08:26.318 --> 00:08:29.568
so that was difficult when it happened,

00:08:31.356 --> 00:08:33.631
but maybe the best thing
that ever happened to me.

00:08:33.631 --> 00:08:36.176
There wouldn't be a Pixar
if that hadn't happened.

00:08:36.176 --> 00:08:39.364
So, you know, you just move on,

00:08:39.364 --> 00:08:40.197
life goes on,

00:08:40.197 --> 00:08:42.835
and you learn from it.

00:08:42.835 --> 00:08:44.761
- [Interviewer] We're at the end here but,

00:08:44.761 --> 00:08:47.165
you were fired from
Apple when you were 30,

00:08:47.165 --> 00:08:49.266
asked to come back when you were what, 42?

00:08:49.266 --> 00:08:50.187
- Mm-hmm.

00:08:50.187 --> 00:08:51.813
- [Interviewer] Did you
think in that moment

00:08:51.813 --> 00:08:53.146
how sweet it is?

00:08:54.241 --> 00:08:56.741
- No, I thought at that moment

00:08:57.854 --> 00:08:59.354
what a, y'know ...

00:09:01.433 --> 00:09:04.288
What a circle of life. (laughs)

00:09:04.288 --> 00:09:08.560
Life is just always
mysterious and surprising,

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:11.401
and you never know what's
around the next corner.

00:09:11.401 --> 00:09:12.234
(bell chimes)

00:09:12.234 --> 00:09:13.067
- Fact number six,

00:09:13.067 --> 00:09:15.009
he was one of the youngest people ever

00:09:15.009 --> 00:09:18.409
to make the Forbes list of
wealthiest people in the world.

00:09:18.409 --> 00:09:19.459
- It's very interesting.

00:09:19.459 --> 00:09:20.626
I was worth...

00:09:23.265 --> 00:09:27.475
about over a million
dollars when I was 23,

00:09:27.475 --> 00:09:30.141
and over 10 million dollars when I was 24,

00:09:30.141 --> 00:09:33.724
and over 100 million
dollars when I was 25.

00:09:36.463 --> 00:09:38.880
And it wasn't that important,

00:09:40.575 --> 00:09:43.658
because I never did it for the money.

00:09:47.370 --> 00:09:49.236
I think money is a wonderful thing

00:09:49.236 --> 00:09:51.640
cuz it enables you to do things,

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:53.470
it enables you to invest in ideas

00:09:53.470 --> 00:09:56.368
that don't have a short-term
payback and things like that,

00:09:56.368 --> 00:09:59.313
but especially at that point in my life

00:09:59.313 --> 00:10:01.635
it was not the most important thing.

00:10:01.635 --> 00:10:03.580
The most important thing was the company,

00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:04.575
the people,

00:10:04.575 --> 00:10:05.941
the products we were making,

00:10:05.941 --> 00:10:07.316
what we were going to enable people to do

00:10:07.316 --> 00:10:08.201
with these products.

00:10:08.201 --> 00:10:11.504
So I didn't think about it a great deal.

00:10:11.504 --> 00:10:13.601
You know, I never sold any stock

00:10:13.601 --> 00:10:16.394
and just really believed that the company

00:10:16.394 --> 00:10:19.530
would do very well over the long term.

00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:20.363
(bell chimes)

00:10:20.363 --> 00:10:21.196
- Fact number seven,

00:10:21.196 --> 00:10:22.029
he was one of the founders

00:10:22.029 --> 00:10:24.720
and was the majority shareholder of Pixar.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:26.957
- This all started with George Lucas.

00:10:26.957 --> 00:10:29.205
He had a problem.

00:10:29.205 --> 00:10:31.092
Now, to describe his problem,

00:10:31.092 --> 00:10:31.925
let me digress a little bit.

00:10:31.925 --> 00:10:35.079
When you make a copy of an
audio cassette tape let's say,

00:10:35.079 --> 00:10:38.009
an analog copy of an audio recording,

00:10:38.009 --> 00:10:40.665
you get noise artifacts, hiss, right?

00:10:40.665 --> 00:10:42.110
We've all experienced that.

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:43.076
Well the same thing happens

00:10:43.076 --> 00:10:47.479
when you make an analog
copy or an optical copy

00:10:47.479 --> 00:10:48.920
of a piece of film.

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:51.250
If you copy optically one
piece of film to another

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:52.679
you get hiss, if you will,

00:10:52.679 --> 00:10:55.065
and in this case the noise
artifacts are visual.

00:10:55.065 --> 00:10:56.834
You get a dirty frame.

00:10:56.834 --> 00:11:00.252
Well, when George was making
the original Star Wars,

00:11:00.252 --> 00:11:03.667
he had to combine many,
many pieces of film together

00:11:03.667 --> 00:11:04.662
to make one frame.

00:11:04.662 --> 00:11:05.820
Some with the models,

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:07.603
some with the matte paintings,

00:11:07.603 --> 00:11:09.010
some with the human characters,

00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:10.561
some with the special effects,

00:11:10.561 --> 00:11:11.659
and by the time he got through

00:11:11.659 --> 00:11:13.070
combining all these pieces of film

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:15.267
to make just one frame of his movie,

00:11:15.267 --> 00:11:16.724
it was dirty.

00:11:16.724 --> 00:11:20.053
And he, being the genius that he was,

00:11:20.053 --> 00:11:24.765
thought, "I wonder if I
could combine them digitally,

00:11:24.765 --> 00:11:26.210
"if they could be totally clean."

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:27.586
Because when you make a digital copy

00:11:27.586 --> 00:11:28.675
of an analog recording,

00:11:28.675 --> 00:11:29.829
it's perfect.

00:11:29.829 --> 00:11:31.831
It turns out if you make a digital copy

00:11:31.831 --> 00:11:34.044
of a piece of film, it's perfect.

00:11:34.044 --> 00:11:34.964
- [Interviewer] Sharper, clearer.

00:11:34.964 --> 00:11:36.819
- Sharper, clearer, without
the noise artifacts.

00:11:36.819 --> 00:11:39.837
So George hired this guy named Ed Catmull,

00:11:39.837 --> 00:11:41.382
who was at the New York
Institute of Technology,

00:11:41.382 --> 00:11:43.163
to come out and build a
computer group for him

00:11:43.163 --> 00:11:44.766
and to figure out how
to solve this problem,

00:11:44.766 --> 00:11:45.935
and they did.

00:11:45.935 --> 00:11:48.505
And George was the first
one to ever do this.

00:11:48.505 --> 00:11:51.177
And after this problem was solved,

00:11:51.177 --> 00:11:54.262
George decided that
he'd solved this problem

00:11:54.262 --> 00:11:57.172
he decided he would sell
this computer group.

00:11:57.172 --> 00:11:59.776
And I ended up buying them.

00:11:59.776 --> 00:12:01.453
I met Ed, and Ed told me his dream

00:12:01.453 --> 00:12:04.674
was to make the world's
first computer-animated film,

00:12:04.674 --> 00:12:06.267
and I bought into that dream,

00:12:06.267 --> 00:12:09.029
both sort of spiritually and financially,

00:12:09.029 --> 00:12:12.196
and together we started Pixar in 1986.

00:12:13.062 --> 00:12:13.987
(bell chimes)

00:12:13.987 --> 00:12:15.023
- Fact number 8,

00:12:15.023 --> 00:12:16.481
he was a Zen Buddhist.

00:12:16.481 --> 00:12:17.999
- At some point, you guys,

00:12:17.999 --> 00:12:18.832
you and Steve Jobs,

00:12:18.832 --> 00:12:21.167
you guys decided to go to India, right?

00:12:21.167 --> 00:12:22.308
- Yes.

00:12:22.308 --> 00:12:23.970
- What was that trip like?

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:24.957
- I didn't have any money.

00:12:24.957 --> 00:12:26.456
I had no travel plans,

00:12:26.456 --> 00:12:29.321
but Steve had started working at Atari

00:12:29.321 --> 00:12:30.644
and he had money.

00:12:30.644 --> 00:12:32.599
A couple thousand dollars.

00:12:32.599 --> 00:12:34.675
And so he offered to buy my ticket.

00:12:34.675 --> 00:12:36.775
I said, "Absolutely, okay, let's go."

00:12:36.775 --> 00:12:38.189
We were just traveling around.

00:12:38.189 --> 00:12:39.932
Just hippies, traveling around.

00:12:39.932 --> 00:12:41.224
We weren't even hippies.

00:12:41.224 --> 00:12:42.292
We shaved our heads.

00:12:42.292 --> 00:12:43.125
(interviewer laughs)

00:12:43.125 --> 00:12:44.292
We were monks.

00:12:45.711 --> 00:12:48.021
We were monk wannabes.

00:12:48.021 --> 00:12:48.974
(bell chimes)

00:12:48.974 --> 00:12:49.807
- Fact number nine,

00:12:49.807 --> 00:12:52.763
him and his friend Steve Wozniak
created illegal blue boxes

00:12:52.763 --> 00:12:54.787
which they used to call the Vatican

00:12:54.787 --> 00:12:56.136
and wake up the Pope.

00:12:56.136 --> 00:12:57.800
- So we built these little boxes

00:12:57.800 --> 00:13:00.191
to do blue boxing, as it was called.

00:13:00.191 --> 00:13:01.867
And we put a little note
in the bottom of 'em,

00:13:01.867 --> 00:13:04.607
our logo was "he's got the
whole world in his hands."

00:13:04.607 --> 00:13:06.509
(laughs)

00:13:06.509 --> 00:13:07.916
And they worked,

00:13:07.916 --> 00:13:09.441
we built the best blue box in the world.

00:13:09.441 --> 00:13:11.639
It was all digital, no adjustments,

00:13:11.639 --> 00:13:14.069
and so you could go up to a pay phone,

00:13:14.069 --> 00:13:17.815
and you could take a
trunk over to White Plains

00:13:17.815 --> 00:13:19.331
and then take a satellite over to Europe

00:13:19.331 --> 00:13:23.199
and then go to Turkey, take
a cable back to Atlanta,

00:13:23.199 --> 00:13:24.519
and you could go around the world.

00:13:24.519 --> 00:13:27.234
You could go around the
world five or six times

00:13:27.234 --> 00:13:28.690
cuz we learned all the codes

00:13:28.690 --> 00:13:30.215
for how to get on the
satellites and stuff.

00:13:30.215 --> 00:13:32.424
And then you could call
the payphone next door,

00:13:32.424 --> 00:13:33.626
and so you could shout in the phone

00:13:33.626 --> 00:13:36.132
and after about a minute it
would come out the other phone.

00:13:36.132 --> 00:13:37.632
It was miraculous.

00:13:39.037 --> 00:13:40.130
And you might ask,

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:41.735
"Well what's so interesting about that?"

00:13:41.735 --> 00:13:44.597
What's so interesting
is that we were young,

00:13:44.597 --> 00:13:46.514
and what we learned was

00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:50.810
that we could build something ourselves

00:13:52.830 --> 00:13:54.330
that could control

00:13:55.746 --> 00:14:00.535
billions of dollars worth of
infrastructure in the world.

00:14:00.535 --> 00:14:01.917
That was what we learned,

00:14:01.917 --> 00:14:03.334
was that us, too,

00:14:05.177 --> 00:14:06.795
y'know, we didn't know much,

00:14:06.795 --> 00:14:09.009
we could build a little thing

00:14:09.009 --> 00:14:12.085
that could control a giant thing.

00:14:12.085 --> 00:14:14.299
And that was an incredible lesson.

00:14:14.299 --> 00:14:16.730
I don't think there would have
ever been an Apple Computer

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:19.057
had there not been blue boxing.

00:14:19.057 --> 00:14:20.661
- [Interviewer] Woz said
you called the Pope.

00:14:20.661 --> 00:14:21.628
- Yeah, we did call the Pope.

00:14:21.628 --> 00:14:25.362
He pretended to be Henry Kissinger.

00:14:25.362 --> 00:14:26.457
Yeah, we got the number of the Vatican,

00:14:26.457 --> 00:14:27.290
and we called the Pope,

00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:29.818
and they started waking people
up in the hierarchy, y'know,

00:14:29.818 --> 00:14:31.437
Cardinals and this and that,

00:14:31.437 --> 00:14:35.156
and they actually sent
someone to wake up the Pope,

00:14:35.156 --> 00:14:37.637
when finally we just burst out laughing

00:14:37.637 --> 00:14:40.832
and they realized that we
weren't Henry Kissinger,

00:14:40.832 --> 00:14:42.388
and so we never got to talk to the Pope,

00:14:42.388 --> 00:14:43.748
but it was very funny.

00:14:43.748 --> 00:14:44.581
- So that's what we got,

00:14:44.581 --> 00:14:46.966
those are the nine surprising
facts about Steve Jobs.

00:14:46.966 --> 00:14:48.488
I hope you enjoyed this video,

00:14:48.488 --> 00:14:50.095
I'd love to know what did you think,

00:14:50.095 --> 00:14:51.871
what fact was the most interesting

00:14:51.871 --> 00:14:53.449
or resonated the most with you,

00:14:53.449 --> 00:14:55.253
please leave it in the comments below

00:14:55.253 --> 00:14:56.923
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:14:56.923 --> 00:14:58.488
Thank you guys again for watching,

00:14:58.488 --> 00:14:59.666
I believe in you,

00:14:59.666 --> 00:15:01.816
hope you continue to believe in yourself,

00:15:01.816 --> 00:15:04.230
and whatever you one word is,

00:15:04.230 --> 00:15:05.300
much love.

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:06.490
See you soon.

00:15:06.490 --> 00:15:08.573
(whoosh)

